text=f'''<header><p class="eyebrow">PISA 2025 · Armenia · Achievement-level redesign · 13 November 2026</p><h1>Household resources, school composition and achievement in Armenia</h1><p class="subtitle">An adaptation of the Brazil–China–Russia education-production framework, with age and grade as controls.</p></header>
<section class="lead"><p><strong>Dropping the grade-overlap requirement restores the main analysis to 5,429 students in 292 schools, or 80.6% of Armenia’s weighted sample.</strong> The question is now how achievement varies with family resources and school context. School mean possessions has a positive conditional association with all three domains: {main_sc.est:.1f} science points, {math_sc.est:.1f} mathematics points and {read_sc.est:.1f} reading points per HOMEPOS unit, holding own possessions fixed. Parental education is examined separately and jointly, with matched-sample checks.</p><p>Schoolmates’ average achievement is strongly associated with individual scores, but contemporaneous peer-score coefficients cannot establish that peers caused the difference. The report distinguishes achievement clustering, background composition and causal peer mechanisms.</p></section>
<section><h2>1. Research question and relationship to the reference paper</h2><p>The supplied PISA 2018 paper examines achievement, between-school variance, socioeconomic school composition and grade-related heterogeneity across Brazil, Chinese jurisdictions and Russian samples. This report retains its education-production perspective and its distinction between family and school context, while changing the target from grade gains to achievement levels in Armenia. It estimates science, mathematics and reading separately.</p><p>Three questions organize the analysis: how much achievement variation lies between schools; how students’ own resources and the socioeconomic composition of their schools relate to achievement; and how individual scores co-vary with the average scores of sampled schoolmates. Age and grade enter as controls. No annual-learning conversion, grade treatment, overlap trimming, or causal forest is used.</p><p>This is a focused adaptation, not an exact replication of every input in the original paper. School infrastructure, staffing and task mastery are not included here. Omitting them preserves the available student sample but means that school composition may proxy those unmeasured inputs. Random intercepts do not remove all sorting or make composition exogenous.</p></section>
<section><h2>2. Sample retention and measures</h2><p>The Armenian file contains 6,804 students. Requiring observed age, grade, sex and home language retains 5,708. Household possessions then retains 5,429. In the previous forest design, the grade 9–10 possessions sample had 5,390 respondents, but requiring at least two respondents per grade within a school reduced it to 1,760; propensity trimming removed only two more. Thus school-grade support, rather than propensity trimming itself, caused the largest additional loss.</p>{coverage}<p>All observed grades are now eligible, and single-grade schools are retained. Requiring parental education does not maximize retention: education alone covers 52.4% of the weighted sample, and possessions plus education covers 51.8%, less than the ESCS model’s 56.9%. The larger possessions model is therefore the primary analysis. Joint and matched-sample models show what changes when parental information is required. No missing SES values are silently set to zero or treated as low resources.</p><p>HOMEPOS measures household possessions on the supplied PISA index scale. PAREDINT is the highest educational attainment of either parent expressed in years, not the father’s education alone. School composition is the student-weighted mean among the relevant model’s analytic respondents. This definition preserves the within/between identity but makes school means sensitive to nonresponse. These are sampled-school composition measures, not a census of classmates.</p><p>Age categories are constructed by rounding 12 × the supplied AGE variable. They approximate months of age; they are not independently reconstructed from an observed test date. Grade is categorical. Sex and home-language status are additional controls. Outcomes remain in PISA points, with all ten plausible values used.</p><h3>Achievement and socioeconomic groups</h3>{fg}<p>The descriptive groups follow the reference paper’s bottom-40%, middle-50%, top-10% convention. Ties remain together, so actual shares differ from those labels. Parental education has only two occupied groups at its 40th/90th-percentile cutpoints (14 and 16 years); an empty upper group is not fabricated. School-mean education has three occupied groups.</p></section>
<section><h2>3. Estimation and within/between interpretation</h2><p>The principal weighted model includes own possessions H and its school mean H̄, with optional own parental education P and its school mean P̄:</p><div class="equation">Yᵢⱼ = α + βHᵢⱼ + γH̄ⱼ + θPᵢⱼ + κP̄ⱼ + age-category effects + grade-category effects + sex/language controls + εᵢⱼ.</div><p>The equivalent centered representation is β(Hᵢⱼ − H̄ⱼ) + (β + γ)H̄ⱼ. Thus β is the own-resource coefficient, β + γ is the between-school coefficient, and γ is the contextual school association holding own resources fixed. The same identity applies to parental education. The variance of the between-school sum is computed directly through replication, retaining covariance.</p><p>A school fixed-effects benchmark estimates own-resource associations while absorbing unrestricted school intercepts, in addition to age and grade effects. It cannot estimate school-constant composition coefficients, so it is not used to claim peer effects. School singleton observations may be removed in that benchmark. Leave-one-out SES specifications exclude the focal student from the composition mean and omit the small number of schools with only one analytic respondent.</p><p>Primary linear inference uses final student weights, all 80 supplied replicate weights, Fay’s factor 0.5, and all ten plausible values. Weighted school means are recomputed in every replicate. The sampling variance for each plausible value is the sum of squared replicate deviations divided by 20; total variance adds 1.1 times the between-plausible-value variance. Reported intervals use the imputation-based t degrees of freedom. They are pointwise intervals, not simultaneous confidence bands; comparisons across specifications are sensitivity checks rather than formal tests of coefficient differences.</p><p>To connect with the original mixed-effects architecture, unweighted REML sensitivities include school random intercepts with age and grade fixed effects. A further sensitivity treats school, monthly age and grade as random intercepts. Null, controls-only and composition models use identical rows within each resource sample. These are model-based sensitivities: lmer’s prior weights are not multilevel survey probability weights. Boundary and convergence diagnostics are retained. Models with fixed age/grade categories and a school random intercept had no singular fits or lme4 convergence messages. The fully random age/grade specification had boundary fits in up to nine of ten plausible values within a sample/domain. This supports retaining fixed age and grade categories as the preferred controls.</p></section>
<section><h2>4. Resources and socioeconomic school context</h2>{fc}{tablecoef(main)}<p>On the same joint-sample students, the science school-possessions coefficient falls from 29.76 to 9.47 when own and school parental education are added; corresponding changes are 39.75 to 17.09 in mathematics and 20.58 to 8.38 in reading. These are specification comparisons, not formal tests of the changes. In the joint model, own parental education remains positively associated with science (3.75 points per year), mathematics (4.68) and reading (3.01). The contextual school-education coefficients are 12.07, 13.15 and 6.75 points per year, respectively, with all three intervals above zero. By contrast, the joint-model possessions coefficients, both own and contextual, have intervals spanning zero. The results therefore distinguish material resources from parental education; they do not support treating the two as interchangeable SES measures. This does not establish that parental education is the causal mechanism.</p><p>Each possessions coefficient is per one HOMEPOS index unit; each education coefficient is per parental-education year. Their raw magnitudes cannot rank the relative importance of the two constructs. Confidence intervals describe conditional associations, not returns to providing assets or raising a parent’s education.</p><p>The larger possessions sample, the possessions-only model on the joint sample, and the joint model serve different purposes. The first preserves coverage. Comparing the first two reveals sensitivity to the selected sample and its school means. Comparing the latter two holds students and possessions means fixed while adding parental education and its composition. Likewise, ESCS and possessions are compared on identical respondents in the appendix; they are never entered together as if they were independent exposures.</p><h3>Between-school variation</h3>{tab(full[['domain','term','Estimate [95% CI]']])}<p>The between_share rows are proportions from an exact final-weighted sample variance partition, averaged over plausible values. They include sampling noise in school means. The mixed-model school shares below estimate a different quantity under a random-intercept model. Neither quantity is a causal measure of school effectiveness or the share of inequality that a school intervention would eliminate.</p>{tab(v[['sample','domain','model','School share (%)']].round(2))}<p>Changes from controls-only to composition models describe how residual variance changes on the same rows. They do not establish that socioeconomic sorting caused the corresponding fraction of the original variance.</p></section>
<section><h2>5. Schoolmates’ scores and peer-background composition</h2><p>For each domain and plausible value, the peer-score measure is the weighted mean score of all other sampled students in the school, including those who lack the focal model’s SES information. The focal student’s score is excluded. This keeps the peer pool broad and avoids direct self-inclusion. The measure is school-wide among sampled PISA-age respondents, not an observed classroom or friendship network.</p>{fp}<p>Peer-score models control own resources, age and grade categories, sex and language. The joint-peer extension adds the socioeconomic school means. A peer-score coefficient is reported per ten points in schoolmates’ average score. No school fixed effects or school random intercept are used for this descriptive score-regression comparison: most school-level score variation would otherwise be absorbed, while leave-one-out variation within a school is mechanically related to the excluded own score.</p><p>The positive score association is consistent with achievement clustering. It can arise from peer influence, selection, common teaching, neighborhood conditions, prior achievement and simultaneous determination. Leaving the focal student out addresses self-inclusion but does not solve the reflection problem. A reduction in the SES-composition coefficient after adding peer scores is not a mediation estimate and should not be interpreted as the fraction transmitted through peers. The primary socioeconomic model therefore remains the model without contemporaneous peer scores.</p><p>Identifying a causal peer mechanism would require additional information such as plausibly exogenous assignment to classes or schools, prior achievement, and a defensible peer group. The current results can describe where disadvantage and low achievement are concentrated, which is relevant to targeting support, without claiming that reallocating classmates would reproduce these coefficients.</p></section>
<section><h2>6. What the redesign supports</h2><p>The earlier grade-overlap failure does not prevent an Armenia study of achievement and school context. It limits a particular grade-treatment comparison. The present approach restores most of the possessions sample, preserves single-grade schools and allows the reference paper’s achievement-level and variance questions to be studied directly.</p><p>Missing parental information remains consequential. A joint-input analysis alone would discard almost half the weighted sample, so it should not replace the larger possessions specification without qualification. Estimates also remain vulnerable to omitted school inputs and nonrandom sorting. The report’s substantive contribution is a transparent separation of household resources, socioeconomic school context and contemporaneous achievement clustering.</p></section>
<section><h2>Appendices and replication</h2><details><summary>A. Fixed-effects and leave-one-out SES checks</summary>{tablecoef(core[core.model.isin(['school_FE','loo_composition'])])}{tablecoef(r[(r.model=='loo_composition')&r.term.str.endswith('_loo')])}{tab(support)}</details><details><summary>B. Matched-sample and ESCS comparisons</summary>{tablecoef(core[(core.model=='composition')&~core['sample'].isin(['possessions','education','joint'])])}</details><details><summary>C. Peer-score model coefficients</summary>{tablecoef(r[r.model.isin(['peer_score','joint_peer'])&r.term.isin(['peer_score','HOMEPOS','HOMEPOS_school','PAREDINT','PAREDINT_school','ESCS','ESCS_school'])])}</details><details><summary>D. Mixed-effect coefficients and diagnostics</summary><p>Unweighted, model-based + PV uncertainty. “Flagged fits” counts any lme4 convergence message, including a singular-boundary notice; it is not a count of optimizer failures. Singular fits indicate boundary variance estimates; random grade effects have few distinct levels and are a sensitivity rather than the preferred control strategy.</p>{tablecoef(mix[mix.term.isin(['HOMEPOS','HOMEPOS_school','PAREDINT','PAREDINT_school'])])}{tab(vc.round(3))}</details><details><summary>E. Selection, group means and reproducibility</summary><p>Selection table: retained-minus-excluded mean achievement for each sample, with BRR/PV intervals. A positive difference indicates that complete respondents have higher observed achievement.</p>{tablecoef(sel.assign(model='retained minus excluded',term='score'))}{tab(g.round(3))}<p>Run scripts 17_armenia_levels.R, 18_armenia_levels_describe.py, 19_armenia_levels_report.py and 20_armenia_levels_validate.py from the repository root. The original extraction is script 02_extract.py. Full coefficients, per-PV estimates, diagnostics and software versions accompany the report. Independent NumPy checks reproduce the main possessions coefficients and their 80-replicate standard errors for all three domains, and verify pooling and variance identities.</p><p>The previous forest report remains at armenia_redesign.html; the regional report remains archived. This active report supersedes their framing for the Armenia achievement-level analysis.</p></details></section>
<section><h2>Sources</h2><ul><li>Avanesian, Garen. <em>How much does a school year add, and for whom? Learning gains and the socio-economic gradient in Brazil, China and Russia.</em> User-supplied PISA 2018 manuscript, epf_eap_preview.pdf, Sections 3–5. Reference material only.</li><li><a href="https://www.oecd.org/en/about/programmes/pisa/how-to-prepare-and-analyse-the-pisa-database.html">OECD: Preparing and analysing the PISA database</a>. Plausible values and replicate-weight inference.</li><li><a href="https://doi.org/10.2307/2298123">Manski (1993), Identification of Endogenous Social Effects: The Reflection Problem</a>. Distinction between endogenous, contextual and correlated social effects.</li><li><a href="https://lme4.github.io/lme4/reference/lmer.html">lme4: lmer documentation</a>. Mixed models and prior-weight interpretation.</li></ul></section>'''
